  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 233 PM CDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nStratus continues to erode from west to east across the CWA this \nafternoon, which was well handled by RRFS/REFS low cloud cover \nproducts. These clouds will eventually clear out of the far eastern \nCWA by early evening. Frontal boundary is also making quick progress \nacross the CWA with winds having turning west or northwest for most \nareas west of I-29. Showers and thunderstorms have developed out \nahead of the front over the eastern CWA, with cores near 20k ft at \ntimes. Although, better ingredients for strong/severe storms reside \nover MN where Marginal/Slight risks are at. High chance PoPs around \n60 percent or greater already exist across the eastern CWA from \nprevious forecast, so no big changes over the eastern areas through \n00Z this evening.\n\nFor tonight, models continue to clear out the CWA rather nicely, \nleaving mostly clear skies and light winds as surface high pressure \nsettles in. Given what appears to be a pretty good radiational \ncooling setup tonight, decided to do a 50/50 NBM/NBM10 blend to \nbring in colder-end temperatures. Most areas should be below \nfreezing tonight, with the James valley getting into the low/mid 20s \nunder the right conditions. There are a few hi-res models hinting at \nfog development late tonight into early Thursday morning in the \nJames and Big Sioux river valleys. Decided to add this to the \nforecast as well given the decent setup for fog.\n\nNext chance for precip looks to be Friday night into Saturday as a \nwarm front lifts northward through the region. NBM probability for \nmeasurable during this time is generally around 30-50 percent across \nthe CWA. Inherited NBM PoPs follow this rather closely, but are \ncloser to 20-40 percent across the area. \n\nIt appears better chances for more widespread rainfall occur Sunday \nnight through Monday night with the arrival of a low pressure \nsystem. Fairly good consistency in the mid-level features amongst \nthe GEFS/ENS/GEPS with a trough moving across the Northern Plains. \nAt the surface, low pressure moves eastward into the Central Plains \nwith an inverted trough poking northward across the Dakotas. \nGEPS/ENS are more bullish with QPF, with probability of a half inch \nor greater at 50-70 percent. GEFS is drier with the system. As of \nnow, precip type looks to be all rain with 850mb temps generally +5C \nto +9C off the Grand Ensemble.",
